// Vector owns a contiguous logical sequence while its policy defines storage
// allocation and value lifetime. Thread and Global policies end individual
// lifetimes before releasing a block. Managed retains replaced objects for its
// Arena, while Const allocates capacity whose slots are already constructed.
//
// A default constructed policy enters the allocation free null state when it
// exposes that constructor. Managed instead requires an Arena and establishes
// its initial reservation immediately.
//
// Copies receive an independent current block. Moves transfer the current block
// and leave their source empty and reusable under its original policy.
//
// Policies form a closed set, but consumers can supply any value type. The
// implementation therefore remains in this header rather than relying on the
// explicit instantiation boundary used by `Container::Bytes`.
template <typename policy_type, typename type>
class Vector {
};

// Vector separates contiguous sequence operations from allocation and value
// lifetime policy. Policy wrappers can therefore impose ownership, reclamation,
// and growth invariants without duplicating the shared sequence logic.
//
// Perimortem defines a closed set of allocation policies containing Global,
// Thread, and Managed. Custom policies require a separate implementation
// because Perimortem supplies no default for them.
//
// Copies receive an independent current block. Moves transfer the current block
// and leave their source empty and reusable under its original policy.
//
// Consumers can supply any value type. The implementation remains in this
// header because that open value set prevents the explicit instantiation
// boundary used by `Container::Bytes`.
template <typename policy_type, typename type>
class Vector {
};

// Vector separates contiguous sequence operations from allocation and value
// lifetime policy. Policy wrappers can therefore impose ownership, reclamation,
// and growth invariants without duplicating the shared sequence logic.
//
// Allocation policies form a closed set. Thread and Global end each value
// lifetime before releasing their blocks. Managed retains replaced objects for
// its Arena. Const supplies constructed capacity only during constant evaluation.
//
// Copies receive an independent current block. Moves transfer the current block
// and leave their source empty and reusable under its original policy.
template <typename policy_type, typename type>
class Vector {
};

// Allocates objects that share one lifetime, allowing fast allocation and
// bulk deallocation.
//
// An arena avoids the bookkeeping overhead of an allocation header for every
// object. This makes it useful when many small objects must be created quickly
// and discarded together, such as a deserialized JSON document or one compiler
// transaction.
//
// `construct()` begins an object's lifetime, but Arena does not call individual
// destructors when it resets. Arena owned objects must therefore release no
// independently owned resources from their destructors. In particular, rented
// Bibliotheca storage must be remitted before the arena is reset or destroyed.
class Arena {
};

// Allocates objects whose access ends together when the Arena resets or is
// destroyed, allowing fast allocation and bulk storage reclamation.
//
// An arena avoids the bookkeeping overhead of an allocation header for every
// object. This makes it useful when many small objects must be created quickly
// and discarded together, such as a deserialized JSON document or one compiler
// transaction.
//
// `construct()` begins a C++ object lifetime in Arena storage. Reset ends every
// access promise without calling individual destructors. A later allocation can
// reuse retained storage and thereby end the former C++ object lifetime. Arena
// values cannot depend on individual destructors to release separate
// allocations, handles or Bibliotheca blocks. Rented Bibliotheca storage must
// therefore be remitted before the Arena is reset or destroyed.
class Arena {
};

// Arena groups objects so reset invalidates every pointer and reference without
// calling individual destructors.
class Arena {
};

